<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bendigo</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bendigo is a city in central Victoria, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, which grew after gold was discovered there in 1851. It became one of the richest goldfields in the world, later mined from deep quartz reefs, and attracted a large Chinese community. Its gold-rush wealth is reflected in its Victorian architecture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bendigo.jpg" alt="Bendigo" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michael Coghlan from Adelaide, Australia · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bendigo_CBD.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bendigo, formerly known as Sandhurst, lies in the Bendigo Valley in north-central Victoria, near the geographical centre of the state and about 150 kilometres north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a>. The Dja Dja Wurrung (Djaara) people are the traditional owners of the area, which they called Leanganook. They used its rich hunting grounds. The city is surrounded by parts of the Greater Bendigo National Park and by the Bendigo Box-Ironbark Region, an Important Bird Area identified for swift parrots and other woodland birds. Its climate is dry and temperate, with warm, variable summers and cool, cloudy winters.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>White settlers established the first of a number of vast sheep runs in 1837 and displaced the Dja Dja Wurrung, who experienced two waves of settlement and dispossession, from the south from 1837 and from the north from 1845. Introduced animals also rooted up the nutritious moon-nar tuber, the yam daisy, which became scarce within a year.</p><p>Gold was officially discovered in October 1851, just after the finds at neighbouring Castlemaine, and some diggers moved from there, bringing the population to 40,000 in less than a year. A number of them were Chinese, and their descendants still live in the region. The discovery on Bendigo Creek turned a sheep station into one of colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>'s largest boomtowns, intensified the Victorian gold rush and drew migrants from around the world, particularly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. In 1852 William Swan Urquhart surveyed the goldfields under the Surveyor General of Victoria, Robert Hoddle. From 1853 to 1891 the town was officially named Sandhurst.</p><p>Bendigo was declared a city in 1871. Its rapid growth caused a water shortage, partly solved by a viaduct from the Coliban River. The architect William Charles Vahland left a mark on it, designing more than 80 buildings, among them the Alexandra Fountain, and giving his name to a popular cottage design. A tram network existed by 1890. The boom lasted until the early 20th century, and after a temporary decline in population and employment, growth resumed from the 1930s as Bendigo became a manufacturing and regional service centre.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The wealth of the gold rush is reflected in the city's late Victorian colonial architecture. Several buildings are on the Victorian Heritage Register, including the Bendigo Town Hall, the Old Post Office and the Old Bendigo Law Courts, which are in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">Second Empire</a> style. Sacred Heart Cathedral, completed in 1977, is the largest church building in provincial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>. The Bendigo Art Gallery is one of Australia's oldest and largest regional art galleries. Tourism centres on the gold-rush history, with attractions such as the Central Deborah Gold Mine, the Bendigo Tramways and the Golden Dragon Museum. The first women's cricket match in Australia was played in Bendigo in 1871.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bendigo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Weeks after separation, gold was found at Ballarat and <b>Bendigo</b>, and the population rose from about 77,000 to over 500,000 within a decade.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-victoria">Colony of Victoria</a></li></ul></section>
